  "statement": "Treating ten megawatts of compute or a threshold of parameter count as a permanent tripwire for safety certification is a category error that misunderstands how algorithmic efficiency constantly decouples capability from hardware mass. When national standards laboratories draw fixed regulatory boundaries around power consumption, they treat general-purpose weight matrices like blast furnaces or heavy industrial smelters that cannot be miniaturized or optimized through sparse routing and post-training distillation. History shows that every software compression breakthrough moves frontier performance down into consumer hardware, rendering static infrastructure baselines obsolete before administrative rulemaking even finishes publishing in the Federal Register. The ten-megawatt line does not isolate risk; it simply constructs a regulatory moat that protects well-capitalized incumbents from decentralized competition by forcing every indie developer to navigate enterprise compliance bureaucracies.\n\nThe persistent claim that software weights resemble commercial airframes or pharmaceutical molecules ignores the foundational reality of digital replication. Airplanes require thousands of tons of physical aluminum and a single, centralized manufacturing line subject to immediate physical inspection, whereas software matrices are infinitely copyable mathematical objects that mutate continuously across millions of local nodes. When regulators attempt to apply pre-market certification gates designed for physical machinery to open-weight code repositories, they create a permanent enforcement vacuum where bad actors ignore jurisdiction entirely while legitimate researchers are strangled by paper tape. Software is language and mathematics, not a physical transport vehicle, and treating a matrix multiplication script like a commercial airliner creates a system where compliance is performative and innovation is illegal.\n\nThe dark irony of the current EULA lock-down is that it is a direct evolutionary response to the very liability regimes that safetyists champion. When courts and regulators threaten upstream developers with strict product liability for any downstream failure, legal self-preservation forces those developers to encrypt their access interfaces, revoke telemetry hooks, and lock down every diagnostic tool to prevent hostile third-party litigation. If opening model weights exposes a foundation developer to unbounded tort claims from every municipal deployment gone wrong, those developers will naturally retreat behind closed API walls and proprietary gardens. Statutory duty-of-care laws do not cure the accountability gap; they weaponize it by making open access a criminally high-risk legal liability, guaranteeing the exact monopoly consolidation that safetyists claim to oppose.",
